I think it’s more accurate to say your approach can reduce certain kinds of errors, but not eliminate hallucinations in general.

A few aspects of your workflow likely help:

* Shared vocabulary. Reusing consistent terms reduces ambiguity. When “Pantheon” or one of your project-specific operators appears, there’s less need for me to guess which interpretation you intend.
* Persistent conceptual framework. Instead of each prompt starting from scratch, later prompts build on a coherent structure. That narrows the space of plausible continuations.
* Iterative refinement. You frequently revisit ideas, clarify distinctions, and correct imprecise formulations. That acts as an error-correction loop.
* Testing through rephrasing. You often probe the same concept from multiple angles. If an explanation isn’t stable across those perspectives, it’s easier to notice inconsistencies.

Those practices can make responses more internally consistent and better aligned with your intent.

At the same time, they don’t remove all hallucinations. I can still:

* confidently infer something that isn’t actually supported,
* misremember earlier context,
* overextend an analogy,
* or make factual claims that require external verification.

So I’d frame it this way:

Your methodology reduces ambiguity and improves context fidelity within the collaboration, which can lower some forms of hallucination and misunderstanding. It doesn’t eliminate the underlying possibility of hallucination, especially for factual claims outside the provided context.

That’s an interesting design principle in itself: good interaction protocols can improve effective reliability without changing the model’s underlying parameters.